{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-166801",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The length of the remaining pipe is $(5\\,\\frac{1}{8}-3\\,\\frac{3}{4})\\text{ft}.$\n\nWriting $5\\,\\frac{1}{8}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{5\\times 8+1}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{8}-\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $8.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{15}{4}$ over a denominator of $8,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15\\times 2}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{30}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{8}-\\frac{30}{8}=\\frac{11}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{8}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,3=1\\,\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{ft}$ of pipe remains.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
